		{
			"collection": "redirects",
			"field": "header",
			"type": "alias",
			"schema": null,
			"meta": {
				"collection": "redirects",
				"field": "header",
				"special": ["alias", "no-data"],
				"interface": "header",
				"options": {
					"title": "Redirect",
					"icon": "alt_route",
					"subtitle": "Create a redirect by absolute or pattern matching routes",
					"help": "<h2>Creating a manual redirect</h2><ol><li><p>Enter the old path in <strong>Redirect origin</strong> and the new path in <strong>Redirect destination</strong>.</p></li><li><p>Select an HTTP status code. Use <code dir=\"ltr\">301</code> for a permanent redirect, or <code>302</code> for temporary.</p></li><li><p>Choose <strong>Exact</strong> or <strong>Pattern</strong> as the <strong>Match type</strong>.</p></li><li><p>Enable <strong>Redirect active</strong> when the redirect should be served.</p></li><li><p>Optionally choose a start or end date.</p></li></ol><h3>Pattern matching</h3><p>Pattern matching lets one redirect cover several similar URLs. Use these formats:</p><ul><li><p><code dir=\"ltr\">/legacy/:slug</code> matches URLs such as <code dir=\"ltr\">/legacy/summer-news</code>. The <code dir=\"ltr\">:slug</code> part can change.</p></li><li><p><code dir=\"ltr\">/:slug?</code> matches a path with an optional final part.</p></li><li><p><code dir=\"ltr\">/files/*</code> matches anything after <code dir=\"ltr\">/files/</code>.</p></li><li><p><code dir=\"ltr\">/files/*?</code> also matches when nothing follows <code dir=\"ltr\">/files/</code>.</p></li><li><p><code dir=\"ltr\">/files/:name.pdf</code> matches PDF files such as <code dir=\"ltr\">/files/guide.pdf</code>.</p></li></ul><p>The destination should use the same changing part. For example:</p><p><code dir=\"ltr\">/legacy/:slug</code> → <code dir=\"ltr\">/news/:slug</code></p><p>This sends <code dir=\"ltr\">/legacy/summer-news</code> to <code dir=\"ltr\">/news/summer-news</code>.</p><p>Enter paths starting with <code dir=\"ltr\">/</code>. Pattern destinations cannot contain query strings or URL fragments. If both an exact redirect and a pattern could match, the exact redirect is used first. When several patterns match, the most specific one is used.</p>",
					"helpDisplayMode": "modal"
				},
				"width": "full"
			}
		},
